@Darren - I live on a lake that is full of ice fishers and their shacks in the winter. Granted, augurs are far more common than saws for cutting hole through which one fishes, but saws are also used, especially for those larger fishing shacks in which you want a nice large hole inside for more than one person to use, to help keep lines separated. Saws make a larger hole with nice, clean edges. They do increase the likelihood of unfortunate accident, though. 

I've also never heard of LOEWE but it was easily solved from the crosses. 

The remaining four were simple gimmes.

@Captain Quahog

It must have been the Hunt Trail because I remember iron grips in places and at the time we understood it was the northern terminus of the AT. But I remember seeing as far ahead and to either side as one could see as we threaded the boulders. 

Then came two climbers bounding down across the tops of the boulders instead of threading them. We gaped, and one called out, "Don't worry! We have on Vibrams!"

@Captain Quahog
Last memory. At one point there was a small path to the right that went to a rock that jutted out from the ridge. I went out, lay down on it with my head over the edge, and closed my eyes. Opened them, felt as though I were in free fall. 

Did that about three times. Felt the wind blowing from forever away. Forest below. My younger sister waiting on the main trail, finally realizing I was just gonna have my moment.

@Captain Quahog

4th memory: On the way to the summit, reaching a green plateau with people everywhere leaning over. Couldn't figure out why until one of us lifted up some of the green and saw the carpet of blueberries on the underside.

Kind of like the field of poppies in The Wizard of Oz--reaching the summit just didn't seem as important as eating blueberries in that moment.

@Captain Quahog thank you for sharing. I have not hiked the 100-mile wilderness.... but I did read the book Lost on a Mountain in Maine as well as climb to the top of an old fire tower on Trout Mountain. The view of Katahdin is spectacular from there. I highly recommend the hike.  

I agree Maine is much more than the coast and I love getting up to Baxter as often as I can.  I usually go to the south entrance but some time I would like to explore Scraggly Lake. 

@Lynn  thank you for sharing your memories.

